00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:06.389 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  mute findings is a powerful volume  management tool within security command  center it allows you to automatically  silence findings based on criteria you  specify or manually silence individual  findings  this feature is relevant for many  potential use cases for example assets  within non-production environments where  some stricter requirements may not be  applicable  recommendations to use customer managed  encryption keys in projects that don't  contain critical data  when granting broad access on a data  store which intentionally is open to the  public and disseminates public  information  or for findings not relevant to your  organization based on your company's  policies  muted findings don't appear by default  in the security command center dashboard  however they continue to be logged and  can be accessed as needed  there are two ways you can mute findings  you can create mute rules that  automatically mute findings as they are  generated  or you can choose to mute and unmute  individual findings  let's see this feature in action  currently we are located at the security  command center findings tab for a  specific project  we'll focus on a category of findings  that are generated when bucket logging  is disabled suppose we want to silence  findings within this category we can use  the mute findings feature to hide them  as you can see we can select individual  findings to mute and unmute using the  mute options menu but you can also  create a mute rule to automatically mute  findings as they are generated based on  a criteria  first we'll give the mute role in id  note that this rule will apply to all  findings generated within the resource  that we have selected  now we'll set up the filter by selecting  category equal to bucket logging  disabled  after clicking apply we can preview any  existing findings that match this filter  once this rule is implemented all  existing and new findings that exactly  match the filter will be hidden and the  mute attribute for the findings will be  set to muted  one thing to note if you are creating  mute rules with the gcloud tool or  security command center api muting  existing and future findings are two  separate operations  so be sure to check out the  documentation for details  after clicking save we'll return to the  security command center and review our  findings  our new mute role has taken effect and  we can no longer see any findings in the  bucket logging disabled category  as mentioned mute rules created in the  console will mute both existing and  future findings that match the filter so  let's create another bucket to see how  future findings are handled  we're back after creating a new bucket  we can see that no additional findings  in the bucket logging disabled category  are visible however findings that are  muted are not deleted and new findings  that match mute rules continue to be  logged as such they can be viewed by  selecting more options and choosing  include muted findings  now we can see the muted findings  including the additional finding that  was just created for our new bucket  the include muted findings option is  useful when you are trying to review all  findings for any audit or compliance  needs  now if we would like to review only the  muted findings we can apply a filter  mute equals muted  in these muted findings we can see when  a finding was muted and who muted the  finding which we can use for audit  purposes at any time we can also  configure any notifications that we've  set up to exclude updates from any new  or existing findings that are muted  mute rules can be managed from the mute  settings page  on this page we can view our rules edit  them and delete them  when we delete a rule it doesn't unmute  any findings that were muted in the past  as a result of this rule but future  findings will no longer be muted  congratulations you're now equipped to  use the mute findings feature and  security command center as a  customizable volume management tool  [Music]  you

